steps.message:
The build succeeds, but there's one warning that ftruncate's return value is unused. Let's handle this properly. If ftruncate fails, the block file's size will be wrong (reads past EOF will return short reads). What should we do on failure? Options: close the fd and return -1 (treat it as a write error), or ignore it. For correctness, failing to set the size is a real problem for reads at the tail end. But note: this only matters for the last block of a non-multiple-of-TREEPAGESIZE export, and for files created by O_CREAT — if ftruncate fails on creation, subsequent pwrite will extend the file anyway (pwrite past EOF extends the file). The actual risk: reads of never-written regions of the tail block will return short. It's an edge case, but the warning must be fixed regardless. Let's handle the error: close and return -1.

Wait, but let me think: what if the file already exists with correct data, and ftruncate fails (e.g., EINTR)? Returning -1 would cause the write to fail unnecessarily. Hmm. Retry on EINTR:

```c
while (ftruncate(fd, blocklen) < 0) {
    if (errno == EINTR) continue;
    close(fd); fd = -1; break;
}
```
This is clean. Note: ftruncate to the same size can also fail on some filesystems (rare). Treat it as an error.

Actually, one more consideration: what about when ftruncate isn't needed, i.e., when the file already exists and blocklen is... we always call it on O_CREAT opens. An ftruncate to the same size is normally a no-op success. OK.
