Wait a sec — there's a subtle point I should verify for the case of moving ftruncate outside the lock: for the tail block of a newly-created odd-sized export... no problem, only one thread is creating it.

But there's one problem: when ftruncate is outside the lock, and a partial READ on the tail block runs concurrently right after the file has been created: the file is created with size 0, ftruncate hasn't been executed yet, and the read sees the short file → pread short/EOF → rawexpread_fully treats ret==0 as failure! The race window is very small, and reads on a block being written are client-side overlaps... but even a READ of the tail region concurrent with the first WRITE to the tail block — is this legal in the NBD protocol? Non-overlapping ranges: a write to [4500,500) and a read of [4900,100) overlap. If the ranges don't overlap, no problem... if you read [4096, 5000) while concurrently writing to [4500,500), they overlap. The client has to serialize. OK, acceptable.

Hmm, actually wait a sec — there's a more general version: any read into a newly-created block (even a full-sized block) before ftruncate: the file size is 0, pread at offset 0..len → EOF → read error! For full blocks, blocklen=4096; before ftruncate, the size is 0. Reads into a block being concurrently written = client overlap. But reads that don't overlap with the write being executed cannot reach that block (data doesn't exist there yet... well, actually, it can: after thread W writes to block A, the client issues a read of block A (ordered); the server processes the write (response), then the read... ftruncate completes during the write processing, before the response. No problem.)

OK, good. Also, note that the `mode & O_CREAT` check now uses fstat — the include is needed: sys/stat.h is already included ✓.

Now — one more consideration about the real cause of the stalls. Let me look at the numbers: treeintegrity alone (09:39): 94 seconds to complete. Full suite #2: failed at around 28 seconds. Full suite #1: failed (unknown time). The suite that passed (the 09:52 run, the pre-refcount one) — that also included treeintegrity and it passed.

Hmm, what if the stalls correlate with the machine being busy due to my own parallel activity? Run #1 (failed at 09:42): nothing else was running. Hmm.

Actually — let me reconsider the stall from a completely different angle: **the server's socket write buffer**. The tester reads responses in a single select loop, and also writes requests. The server's mainloop reads requests serially: `socket_read(client, req)` is blocking; WRITE data is read inline before pushing to the pool. If a worker tries to write a response while the mainloop is blocked on socket_read for the next request's data... no, that's fine, they're separate fds/directions.

How about this: the tester's write side stops (blocked flag due to in-flight overlap), 34 requests in flight; the server processes them; the responses get written via socket_write under client->lock... 4 workers contending for client->lock to write responses — serialization but fast.

The jbd2 stall is real (observed in D state). Reducing metadata pressure is the lever. My truncate fix helps reopens. Let me also test: bump the cache size to 16384 to eliminate eviction churn in this test (12800 blocks all cacheable). Combined effect: after the initial 12800 creations, zero opens, zero mkdirs, zero truncates — everything cached. This should dramatically smooth things out. Risk: fd usage of 12800 — no problem on this machine; on machines with RLIMIT 1024, we'll get EMFILE at around 1000 fds → writes fail. Hmm, that risk exists anyway with the cap of 8192. Actually — let's handle EMFILE gracefully: on open failure with EMFILE/ENFILE, evict one and retry! This makes the cache self-adaptive under fd pressure. Add to treefile_getfd's miss path:

```c
fd = open_treefile(...);
if (fd < 0 && (errno == EMFILE || errno == ENFILE)) {
	pthread_mutex_lock(lock);
	treefile_evict_one(client);
	pthread_mutex_unlock(lock);
	fd = open_treefile(...);
}
```
With the refcnt protection, evict_one closes a truly unused fd. If the cache is all in use (rare), the open fails → error. With this graceful degradation, I can confidently set the cap to 16384.

Let me add the EMFILE retry and bump the cap.
Let's add one more fallback: when fd is exhausted (EMFILE/ENFILE), first reclaim one unused entry and then retry. This way, we can relax the cache limit to 16384 (the 50MB test has 12800 blocks, which fits entirely, and there's zero jitter from reopen).

